From: xPDFSDK team <noreply@xpdfsdk.local>
Date: 2026-06-03
Subject: [PATCH ship-7/7] Veloce deeper render-abort checkpoints

Background
==========
Patch 06 added `FPDFEx_SetRenderAbort()` and checked the abort flag at
`CPDF_RenderStatus::RenderObjectList()` page-object boundaries. That is
enough when a page contains many small top-level objects, but not when a
single top-level object is a large Form XObject, transparency group, image,
or text/path object. In that case PDFium can spend seconds inside
`RenderSingleObject()` after xPDFSDK has already requested cancellation.

This patch
==========
Adds abort checks inside the expensive per-object render path:

  * before `RenderSingleObject()` starts object work,
  * after visibility / clip / transparency phase boundaries,
  * before and after `ProcessObjectNoClip()`,
  * inside `ContinueSingleObject()` for progressive/image continuation,
  * before Form XObject recursion and after the recursive object list,
  * after transparency offscreen rendering and soft-mask rendering,
  * after Type3 glyph Form rendering,
  * after soft-mask Form rendering.

The checks are phase-boundary checks, not per-pixel checks. They keep the
patch low-risk and preserve PDFium's internal invariants while making
cancellation observe more than just top-level page-object boundaries.

Expected interpretation
=======================
Measure cancel-request to native-return time on error.pdf pages 2/3.
If this patch drops cancellation latency from multi-second to near the
watchdog budget, the slow path was Form/transparency recursion below
`RenderSingleObject()`. If cancellation is still multi-second, the hot spot
is inside one lower-level primitive such as image decode/composite, path
stroking/fill, glyph rendering, or bitmap blending; patch 08 should add
bounded checks in that specific primitive.

